{
  "findings": [
    {
      "error": "The summary card teaches the classic p-value misreading. A p-value of 0.26 is the chance of a gap this big or bigger if the buttons truly sell the same. It is not the chance that the gap 'is just luck.' The AI's own Day 1 reply states the conditional meaning correctly, but the card a learner keeps states it wrongly.",
      "evidence": "\"translate terms like p-value into everyday odds—such as a 1-in-4 chance that the gap is just luck.\" vs. {{ai}}: \"If both buttons actually sell at the same rate, random luck alone creates a gap this big or bigger about 26% of the time\"",
      "location": "summary card",
      "path": "all"
    },
    {
      "error": "The p-value is presented as the probability that luck explains the observed gap, or that the lead 'is luck' or 'was just luck.' A p-value of 0.33 is the chance of a gap this large if there is no real difference. It is not the chance that the lead is luck or that you are being fooled. Nothing on these paths corrects this, and the strong choice and its notes endorse it.",
      "evidence": "stop-friday note: \"leaving a 1-in-3 chance that a 10-sale gap is just luck\"; extend-to-full: \"there is still a 1-in-3 chance the 10-sale lead is luck\" / \"A 1-in-3 chance of being fooled is still too high\"; ship-green reply: \"with a 1-in-3 chance that this lead was just luck\"; ask-ai-chance reply: \"wait until luck has less than a 5% chance of explaining the gap\"",
      "location": "decision sample-check, choice stop-friday note; decision final-call-early, choice extend-to-full and ship-green-friday; decision day1-check, choice ask-ai-chance, reply from {{data}}",
      "path": "all paths through stop-friday or final-call-early, and all paths through ask-ai-chance"
    },
    {
      "error": "A non-significant result (p≈0.86) is taught as proof that the buttons perform the same. A non-significant result only fails to show a difference. With 3,500 per arm, the confidence interval still allows a lift of roughly ±0.9 percentage points, which is about a 20% relative change. So 'perform the same,' 'no real difference' and 'color didn't change shopper behavior' are not supported. This is presented as the strong lesson.",
      "evidence": "\"You recognized that a 3-purchase difference across 7,000 visitors means the two buttons perform the same.\"; panel note \"Chance of luck: 84% (no real difference)\"; \"so color didn't change shopper behavior\"; \"shoppers don't actually care\"",
      "location": "decision final-call-full, choice keep-blue-clear note and reply; choice claim-blue-beats-green note; sample-check full-3500 panel note",
      "path": "Paths 5, 6, 11, 12, 17, 18, 23, 24, 29, 30, 35, 36, 41, 42"
    },
    {
      "error": "The sample-size figure is too low. Detecting 4.0% vs. 5.2% at the usual 5% two-sided significance and 80% power needs about 4,800 visitors per button: (1.96+0.84)²×(0.04·0.96+0.052·0.948)/0.012² ≈ 4,775. At 3,500 per button, power is only about 67%, so this is not 'reliably' spotting the lift.",
      "evidence": "\"To reliably spot a real jump from 4.0% to 5.2% (30% more sales) without getting fooled by luck, we need **3,500 visitors per button**\"",
      "location": "decision sample-check, message from {{ai}}",
      "path": "all"
    },
    {
      "error": "Differences between two rates are labeled as percent changes when they are percentage-point gaps. 6.5% vs. 4.0% is +2.5 percentage points, which is a 62.5% relative increase. The same mislabel appears for 0.8 and 0.1.",
      "evidence": "\"Difference: +2.5% (5 extra purchases)\"; \"Difference: +0.8% (10 extra purchases)\"; \"Declare that the blue button beats green by 0.1%\"",
      "location": "opening panel; sample-check panels; final-call-full choice claim-blue-beats-green",
      "path": "all"
    },
    {
      "error": "The ending credits the learner with saving the team from chasing the Day 1 streak. On these paths, the learner chose to launch green on the Day 1 numbers, and {{data}} stepped in to stop it.",
      "evidence": "\"You saved the team from chasing a Day 1 lucky streak\" vs. chosen action \"Agree with {{product}} and launch the green button now so we don't miss evening sales.\"",
      "location": "ending ending-solid",
      "path": "Paths 5 and 6"
    }
  ]
}

